<DocsLayout title="Cognitive Schemas" description="How human cognitive schemas organize lower-level memories, how to design schemas that guide AI agents, and concrete patterns for software architecture, research, and planning.">
  <h1>Cognitive Schemas</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A cognitive schema is a high-level mental model that organizes many lower-level memories under one
    idea — and tells you when to apply it. Engrams gives your agent the same machinery: a confirmed schema
    bundles related decisions, patterns, and code under a single summary that states <em>when it fires</em>
    and <em>what to do</em>, so the agent loads the model instead of re-reading the history.
  </p>

  <div class="callout tip reveal">
    <strong>The retrieval rule:</strong> Raw facts cost tokens on every session; a schema costs tokens once per activation.
    Because the summary starts with a trigger condition, <code>advise</code> and <code>prime</code> can decide
    <em>before any file is opened</em> whether this situation is one the project has already reasoned about.
  </div>

  <h2 class="reveal">How human schemas organize memory</h2>
  <p>
    Cognitive psychology describes memory as layered. At the bottom sit individual episodes and facts;
    at the top sit <strong>schemas</strong> — abstract frames like <em>code review</em> or <em>restaurant visit</em> that
    group those episodes together. A schema does two things:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>It organizes.</strong> Specific memories become instances of the schema. You do not store
      <em>restaurant visit #47</em> from scratch; you store what was different from the frame, and the frame
      supplies the rest.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It activates conditionally.</strong> A schema carries an activation trigger — cues in the environment
      (<em>a menu, a pull request diff</em>) — plus procedural rules for what to do once it fires. Recall is top-down:
      the cue selects the schema, and the schema selects which lower-level memories matter.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    This is why humans never search their memory linearly. The right frame arriving early is what makes
    behavior fast and consistent — and it is exactly what an LLM agent lacks when it greps a repository cold.
  </p>

  <h2 class="reveal">How schemas guide LLM retrieval in Engrams</h2>
  <p>
    An Engrams schema is a confirmed graph node whose <strong>members</strong> are lower-level memories —
    decisions, system patterns, code nodes, progress entries, custom data, even nested schemas. The schema's
    summary is written as a conditional sentence:
  </p>
  <pre><code>Fires when &lt;trigger condition&gt;: &lt;operational rule&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Every retrieval surface reads that sentence differently:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>How the schema guides it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>engrams advise &lt;paths&gt;</code></td>
        <td>
          Resolves active schemas whose member anchors touch the target paths and prints them in a
          <code>schemas</code> block <strong>above</strong> low-level constraints — the rule arrives before the fine print.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>engrams prime --paths --tags</code></td>
        <td>
          Ranks schemas that match the target paths or tags above unanchored schemas, each entry carrying
          its operational rule summary.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>engrams schema show &lt;target&gt;</code></td>
        <td>
          Returns the schema plus every member enriched with type-specific details (summaries, descriptions,
          file anchors), capped at 20 members with a truncation flag for large clusters.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>engrams brief schema:&lt;id&gt;</code></td>
        <td>One call returns the schema summary, its kind, and enriched details for all immediate members.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The trigger is what makes this work for an LLM: matching a short conditional sentence against the current
    prompt or edited paths is cheap, while the rule is a directive the model can comply with immediately —
    no file reads required. Full-text search (<code>schemas_fts</code>) indexes the whole summary, so triggers
    and rules are findable from any query surface.
  </p>

  <h2 class="reveal">Designing a schema that guides an agent</h2>
  <p>
    A schema is only as good as its summary. Engrams enforces the shape — <code>schema refine</code> accepts
    a structured pair and rejects free-text summaries that lack a conditional trigger:
  </p>
  <pre><code># Structured authoring (recommended) — formats the summary for you
engrams schema refine &lt;target&gt; --trigger "editing public documentation" \
                              --rule "use problem-first risk messaging"
# =&gt; summary: "Fires when editing public documentation: use problem-first risk messaging."

# Manual summaries must begin with a conditional trigger phrase
engrams schema refine &lt;target&gt; --summary "Fires when ...: ..."
# Accepted openers: "Fires when", "When", "Applies if", "Activates when"</code></pre>
  <div class="callout warn reveal">
    <strong>Validation is deliberate:</strong> <code>--summary</code> strings without a trigger condition are rejected
    with guidance on the required format, and there is no bypass flag. A schema that cannot say when it fires
    can never be activated by <code>advise</code> or <code>prime</code>.
  </div>
  <h3 class="reveal">Authoring principles</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Write the trigger as observable context.</strong> A file path, a file kind, a task verb — something
      present in the prompt or the paths being edited. <em>“When working on things”</em> never matches;
      <em>“Fires when editing files under src/ops/”</em> always does.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Write the rule as an imperative.</strong> The agent must be able to comply without more reading.
      <em>“Error handling matters here”</em> is a description; <em>“return the AppError envelope and never
      unwrap in library code”</em> is a rule.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anchor members where the rule applies.</strong> <code>advise</code> and <code>prime</code> match through
      member anchors and centroid tags — an unanchored schema never activates on a path.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Members first, summary second.</strong> Let <code>schema scan</code> cluster related nodes, confirm
      the cluster, then <code>refine</code> the summary. The schema should summarize memory that exists,
      not invent memory you wish you had.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One schema per recurring situation.</strong> If two triggers always fire together, they are one
      schema; if one trigger demands two unrelated rules, it is two schemas.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="reveal">Example: software architecture</h2>
  <p>
    A cluster of error-handling decisions, a result-envelope pattern, and the central error type become one
    schema that fires on any fallible-function work:
  </p>
  <pre><code>engrams schema refine error-envelope \
  --trigger "adding or editing a fallible public function in Rust code" \
  --rule "return the AppError envelope and never unwrap outside tests"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Running <code>engrams schema show error-envelope</code> returns the schema plus its enriched members:
  </p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "status": "success",
  "schema": &#123;
    "id": 4,
    "name": "error-envelope",
    "summary": "Fires when adding or editing a fallible public function in Rust code: return the AppError envelope and never unwrap outside tests.",
    "summary_source": "agent",
    "status": "confirmed",
    "members": [
      &#123;
        "kind": "system_pattern", "id": 12, "name": "result-envelope",
        "description": "Public fallible functions return Result&lt;T, AppError&gt;.",
        "severity": "error", "anchors": ["src/ops"]
      &#125;,
      &#123;
        "kind": "decision", "id": 58,
        "summary": "Ban unwrap in library code; convert with AppError::from",
        "status": "active", "anchors": ["src/ops/schemas/list.rs"]
      &#125;,
      &#123;
        "kind": "code", "id": 91, "path": "src/errors.rs",
        "symbol": "AppError", "line_count": 64
      &#125;
    ],
    "member_count": 3,
    "truncated": false
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    From now on, <code>engrams advise src/errors.rs</code> surfaces this schema above the constraint list, and
    <code>engrams prime --paths src/ops</code> ranks it at the top of the schemas block — the agent reads the rule
    before it reads the code.
  </p>

  <h2 class="reveal">Example: research</h2>
  <p>
    Research findings stored as custom data tend to go stale because nothing forces an agent to consult them.
    A schema turns the survey into a standing rule:
  </p>
  <pre><code>engrams schema refine survey-then-decide \
  --trigger "evaluating a new dependency or settling an open design question" \
  --rule "read the saved research findings first and cite the finding ID in the decision"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Members: the custom-data entries holding the survey findings (each enriched with its <code>key</code> and
    full <code>value</code> — JSON stored as JSON), plus the decision that turned the survey into a roadmap. Now a prompt like
    <em>“should we adopt bgk for clustering?”</em> activates the schema through <code>advise</code> — the agent is
    told to ground the answer in finding IDs instead of improvising from training-data priors.
  </p>

  <h2 class="reveal">Example: planning</h2>
  <p>
    Planning drift happens when every session re-derives what to build next from a backlog of raw notes.
    The <code>what-to-build-next</code> schema packages the survey, the roadmap decision, and the session notes
    into one activation:
  </p>
  <pre><code>engrams schema refine what-to-build-next \
  --trigger "planning a release or choosing what to build next" \
  --rule "pick from the tiered version-by-version roadmap before inventing new scope"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Running <code>engrams prime --tags roadmap</code> ranks this schema first with its rule inline, so planning
    sessions start from the committed sequence rather than the loudest recent note. Tagging the schema
    (or its members) with the tags your planning prompts use is what makes the match — anchors for paths,
    tags for topics.
  </p>

  <h2 class="reveal">The loop closes itself</h2>
  <p>
    You do not have to remember to look for schemas. Detection runs continuously in the background,
    and <code>engrams session close</code> tells you two things at the exact moment a session ends:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>schema_opportunities</strong> — candidate clusters that passed the formation gates and
      do not overlap any schema you already confirmed. Each one arrives with a suggested name and a
      ready-to-run <code>engrams schema confirm</code> command. Run it, or don't: an unchanged
      candidate is not nagged about again, and one that keeps growing comes back louder.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>schema_value</strong> — how many retrievals this session were guided by schemas, and
      how many distinct schemas did the guiding. This is the payoff made visible: schemas are doing
      work, here is the count.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The same counter shows up per schema in <code>engrams schema list</code> and
    <code>schema show</code> as the <code>guided</code> field — so a schema that never fires is
    visible too, and worth refining or retiring.
  </p>

  <div class="callout note reveal">
    <strong>The loop:</strong> <code>schema scan</code> finds the cluster, <code>schema confirm</code> seals it,
    <code>schema refine</code> names the trigger and rule, and <code>advise</code> / <code>prime</code> deliver the rule
    exactly when the trigger appears. Each session that follows the rule without re-reading the underlying
    memories is the compounding payoff.
  </div>
